[**ARTICLE XVI CONSTRUCTION AND TERMS	21**](#construction-and-terms)

1. # **NAME AND PURPOSE** {#name-and-purpose}

   1. ## Name {#name}

The name of the organization shall be the Ronin Institute for Independent Scholarship 2.0 (“RIIS 2.0”). RIIS 2.0 shall be a nonprofit organization incorporated under the laws of the State of California and organized under California’s Nonprofit Public Benefit Corporation Law (CA Corp. Code § 5110 *et seq.*) (“CNCL”).

2. ## IRC Section 501(c)(3) Purposes {#irc-section-501(c)(3)-purposes}

RIIS 2.0 is organized exclusively for charitable, scholarly, scientific, and educational purposes. The specific objectives and purposes are to be a venue for our researchers in their execution and dissemination of their scholarly research in the public interest. RIIS 2.0 provides scholarly researchers with membership  affiliation, community, and dissemination opportunities. RIIS 2.0 does not itself conduct, sponsor, or direct research as an institution. The Council may in the future choose to expand the institute’s activities or mandate through revisions to these bylaws and associated policies.

RIIS 2.0 offers an inclusive academic community of belonging to scholars committed to truth and empathy to share ideas and grow intellectually, to improve and develop their capabilities, and to help scholars establish new contacts and collaborations. 

RIIS 2.0 is a platform outside of traditional academia that invites scholars from around the world with unique perspectives to share their voices and address issues in their purview, fostering opportunities for unique academic insights. Scholars with a broad range of experiences can contribute to scientific research for public benefit within the environment of independent scholarship that RIIS 2.0 provides.

3. ## RIIS 2.0 Values And Principles: {#riis-2.0-values-and-principles:}

   1) **Values: Truth and Empathy**

Scholars (both Fellows and Associates) and Staff shall read and understand our founding values of truth and empathy and abide by the specific policies that flow from those values.

Scholars from all disciplines shall seek truths in pursuant to a better understanding of our world. Scholars pursue their research in the way that works best for them. We expect that Scholars will be honest and thoughtful, both in scholarship and in their interactions with the community and world at large.

Empathy demands that one act in good faith and assume good faith from others. RIIS 2.0 expects Scholars to interact in a spirit of kindness, generosity, and patience. We are a highly interdisciplinary and international community with Fellows who may have very different perspectives and backgrounds.

2) **Nondiscrimination**

RIIS 2.0 and its officers, Councilors, employees, and persons served by RIIS 2.0 shall be selected in a non-discriminatory manner with respect to race, color, sex, language, religion, political or other opinion, national or social origin, property, birth or other status such as disability, age, marital and family status, sexual orientation and gender identity, health status, place of residence, and economic and social situation in accordance with local, state and federal law.

3) **Belonging**

RIIS 2.0 and its Scholars, staff, and Council are also committed to incorporating the values of our Belonging Policy in the governance and operations of this Institute. RIIS 2.0 believes everyone has the right to work in a professional environment where their knowledge, skills, and abilities are the factors for their success. RIIS 2.0 expects all affiliated with the organization to maintain standards of propriety, treat everyone professionally, and act without bias. RIIS 2.0 has a zero-tolerance policy for sexual harassment. These values shall be adopted by the Fellows and Associates and be codified in a Belonging Policy adopted by the Council.

RIIS 2.0 acknowledges the values expressed in the Belonging Policy as foundational principles of the Institute. RIIS 2.0 seeks to expand the academic landscape by providing a heterogeneous body of scholars with equitable access to opportunities, resources, and support.

2. # **FELLOWSHIP** {#fellowship}

   1. ## Definition {#definition}

A Fellow must fulfill membership requirements as outlined below and in the policies and procedures documentation adopted from time to time by the governing body of RIIS 2.0 (such body, the “**Council**”, and such documentation, the “**Policies and Procedures**”). 

2. ## Eligibility for Fellowship {#eligibility-for-fellowship}

To be considered for membership, an applicant must demonstrate academic achievement and scholarly commitment, the determination of which shall be at the recommendation of the Affiliation Working Group and, ultimately, the approval of the Council. Applications for affiliation shall be open to any scholar in pursuit of academic scholarship. All Fellows agree to follow the code of conduct and ethics. Fellowship is granted after the completion and receipt of a membership application, payment of annual dues, and recommendation to the Council by the Affiliation Working Group. The Council shall oversee the membership enrollment process.

3. ## Annual Dues {#annual-dues}

Fellowship requires the payment of annual dues. Continued membership is contingent upon being up-to-date on membership dues. Procedures for determining dues will be defined within the Policies and Procedures, with any changes approved by a vote of the membership. A copy of the current dues will be available on the website.

4. ## Rights of Fellows {#rights-of-fellows}

Fellowship shall entitle Fellows to (i) participate in the programs of RIIS 2.0; (ii) have the right to vote on the election or removal of Councilors, on the disposition of all or substantially all of the assets of the corporation, on any merger and its principal terms and any amendment of those terms, and on any election to dissolve the corporation, and to participate in any votes put to the membership by the Council; and (iii) to request RIIS 2.0 to administer the Fellow’s awards, grants and contracts.

5. ## Resignation and Termination {#resignation-and-termination}

   1) **Resignation**

Any Fellow may resign by submitting a resignation via a process overseen by the Secretary. Resignation shall not relieve a Fellow of any dues previously accrued.

2) **Termination**

Termination may occur as a result of failure to meet eligibility requirements, inactivity, or conduct detrimental to RIIS 2.0’s mission and values. In the event that the Council resolves to terminate a Fellow, the Fellow will be given 15 days’ prior notice of the termination and the reasons therefor; as well as an opportunity to be heard, orally or in writing, before the Council, not less than five days before the effective date of the termination.

1) Grounds for Termination

   1) Inactivity

Fellows who do not maintain active participation as defined in these Bylaws and in RIIS 2.0’s Policies and Procedures may have their membership terminated. This includes failure to meet volunteer or dues obligations. Activity might include participation in various virtual gatherings, meetings, working groups, voting, and participation in discussions on online platforms or via email.

2) Ineligibility

If a Fellow no longer meets the qualifications for fellowship (as outlined in the eligibility criteria of the Bylaws), their affiliation will be subject to termination.

3) Violations of Conduct

Fellowship or Associateship may be terminated based on the good-faith determination by the Council at the recommendation of the Affiliation Working Group that a scholar has violated the Code of Conduct or engaged in behavior materially prejudicial to RIIS 2.0’s interests.

3. # **MEETING NOTICES** {#meeting-notices}

   1. ## Membership Meetings {#membership-meetings}

All meeting notices shall be sent out by the Secretary or their designee. Notice of members’ meetings shall be sent no more than 90 days and no less than: 

1) 30 days prior to the Annual Meeting; and

   2) 15 days prior to a Special Meeting. Additionally, if a Special Meeting is requested by any person entitled to call such a meeting other than the Council itself, the Council shall cause notice of such meeting to be given promptly, and, in any case, within 20 days of receipt of the request.

   2. ## Council Meetings {#council-meetings}

Regular meetings of the Council may be held without notice if the time and place of the meetings are fixed in advance by the Council. Special meetings of the Council may be held upon 48 hours' notice delivered personally to each Council member by electronic transmission.

4. # **MEETINGS OF FELLOWS** {#meetings-of-fellows}

   1. ## Annual Meetings {#annual-meetings}

A general virtual meeting of the Fellows shall take place annually (the “Annual Meeting”). The specific date, time, and virtual location of the Annual Meeting will be designated by the Council. At the Annual Meeting, the announcement of the results of the election of new Councilors will be made, and Fellows will receive reports on the activities of RIIS 2.0, and determine the direction of RIIS 2.0 for the coming year.

2. ## Special Meetings {#special-meetings}

   1) The Council, the Chair of the Council, or a petition signed by a greater of five (5) voting Fellows or ten percent of Fellows may call a Special Meeting for any purpose at any time consistent with applicable law.

      2) The Council shall cause the meeting to take place no sooner than 35 days and no later than 90 days after the request is made.

      3) No business other than the business that was set forth in the Notice of the meeting may be transacted at a Special Meeting.

   3. ## Action by Written Ballot in Lieu of a Meeting {#action-by-written-ballot-in-lieu-of-a-meeting}

Subject to approval by the Council, any action which may be taken at any Annual Meeting or Special Meeting may be taken without a meeting. The Council shall cause a written ballot setting forth the proposed action and any related materials to be distributed to all Fellows entitled to vote on the matter no later than 15 days prior to the deadline for submission of such Fellows’ votes.

4. ## Voting {#voting}

Except as otherwise provided in these Bylaws, all matters requiring a vote of the Fellows shall be decided by a simple majority of those casting electronic ballots, provided that quorum is achieved. A quorum of Fellows is achieved when at least 10% of all Fellows or 15 Fellows entitled to vote, whichever is greater, have participated in the vote. All Fellows shall be invited to vote, and those who do not submit a ballot within the prescribed two-week voting period shall be deemed to have abstained. 

5. ## Rules of Order {#rules-of-order}

Should any question of procedure arise at any meeting of the Fellows, the Policies and Procedures shall govern, insofar as such rules are not inconsistent with or in conflict with the Articles of Incorporation, these Bylaws, or with provisions of law.

5. # **ELECTRONIC MEETINGS** {#electronic-meetings}

All Fellow, Council, and Working Group meetings shall be conducted through the use of internet meeting services that support the meeting Policies and Procedures.

6. # **NON-VOTING ASSOCIATES** {#non-voting-associates}

   1. ## General Provisions {#general-provisions}

To allow scholars who are not in a position to commit either time or money to membership to be associated with RIIS 2.0, the Council may admit non-voting Associates to use RIIS 2.0 affiliation and participate in non-voting aspects of the community. For avoidance of doubt, Associates are not “members” for purposes of CNCL Section 5056\.

2. ## Process and Role {#process-and-role}

To become an Associate, an application must be filed. The requirements to be an Associate are the same as those of Fellows, except that Associates do not need to pay full dues. Associates are bound by the same code of conduct as Fellows.

Associates may attend but not vote or participate in Council meetings, and may attend but not vote at Fellowship meetings. However, they may attend and participate in Working Group meetings, other meetings and seminars, and online forums at the Council's discretion.

3. ## Resignation {#resignation}

Any Associate may resign or have their association terminated by the same process as Fellows, *provided, however*, that, at the discretion of the Council, Associates may not be given notice nor an opportunity to be heard by the Council before termination.

7. #    **GOVERNING BODY** {#governing-body}

   1. ## Council role, size, and compensation {#council-role,-size,-and-compensation}

The Council is the governing body of RIIS 2.0, and its Councilors serve as the “board of directors” for purposes of CNCL Section 5210\. It is responsible for the execution of the overall policy and direction of the association and may delegate responsibility for day-to-day operations to staff and working groups. The Council shall have up to fifteen (15), but no fewer than three (3) Fellows; the exact authorized number to be fixed, within these limits, by resolution of the Council.

The Council shall be empowered to appoint Officers with duties described in Section 6 of this Article.

2. ## Terms {#terms}

Elected Councilors will serve for 36 months, with the term beginning at the annual meeting during or immediately following their election. One-third (1/3) of the Council (at least 2 Councilors) shall be renewed every 12 months. Nominations for incoming Councilors shall take place within the twelve months prior to the upcoming annual meeting. Voting for nominated Councilors shall take place in the immediate 6 months prior to the upcoming annual meeting.

There is no term limit on serving as a Councilor.

3. ## Qualifications and Election Procedures {#qualifications-and-election-procedures}

Any Fellow may nominate another Fellow, who has been a Fellow for a minimum of a year or has played a leadership role in a comparable organization, as a candidate to the list of nominees for the Council. Nominees shall be natural persons of the age of majority in their home jurisdiction, such as state, province, or country of residence.

Nominations will be collected by a Volunteer Coordination Working Group.

4. ## Council Elections {#council-elections}

Councilors will be elected by the Fellows as specified in Policy and Procedures.

Elections shall be held annually or as otherwise specified in the Policies and Procedures. The Volunteer Coordination Working Group or the current Council, if this Working Group is not constituted or functioning, shall issue a call for Councilor candidates at least 120 days ahead of the elections. The Volunteer Coordination Working Group shall recruit nominations based on the representation considerations in the Belonging and Volunteer Coordination Policies and Procedures and publish a list of nominated candidates.

Elections are by a vote of the Fellows on all candidates nominated by the deadline. 

For the first election, one-third of the Councilors shall hold office for 3 years, one-third for 2 years, and the remaining third for one year. If the total number of Councilors is not divisible by three, partition into thirds will be done approximately, allocating more persons to the shorter terms; for example, if the total number is 11, the partition shall be 4 persons for one year, 4 persons for two years, and 3 persons for three years. Thereafter, each newly elected Councilor shall serve 3 years. Elections shall be by secret ballot and conducted securely online.

5. ## Councilors and General Duties {#councilors-and-general-duties}

Their general duties are as follows:

1) Perform any duties imposed on them collectively or individually by law, by the Articles of Incorporation, or by these Bylaws;

   2) Appoint and remove, employ and discharge, and, except as otherwise provided in these Bylaws, prescribe the duties and fix the compensation, if any, of all officers, agents, and employees of RIIS 2.0;

      3) Supervise all officers, agents, and employees of the corporation to ensure that their duties are performed properly;

      4) Meet at such times and places as required by these Bylaws or by resolution of the Council.

      5) Register their email addresses with the Secretary, and notices of meetings emailed to them at such email addresses shall be valid notices thereof.

   6. ## Officer Roles and Duties {#officer-roles-and-duties}

There shall be at least three Officers on the Council, consisting of a Chair, Secretary, and Treasurer. The Past Chair shall assist the current principal officers, as needed. Other Officers may be appointed by the Council as needed.

Officers, other than the Past Chair, are selected from among current Councilors by the Council every year and serve 1-year terms in their role, but may be re-appointed by the Council. There is a 3-year term limit to serve as Chair. A past Chair may serve another term as Chair after three (3) years of not serving as Chair. No Councilor may hold more than one Officer role at a time, except as a temporary necessity for up to 6 months.

1) **Chair**

The Chair shall be responsible for convening regularly scheduled Council meetings and shall preside or arrange for another Councilor to preside at each meeting of the Council and at all meetings of the Fellows.

The Chair shall be the chief executive officer of the corporation in the event that the position is vacant. The Chair shall perform all duties as may be required by law, by the Articles of Incorporation, or by these Bylaws, or which may be prescribed from time to time by the Council.

2) **Secretary**

The Secretary shall perform all duties as may be required by law, by the Articles of Incorporation, other relevant sections of these Bylaws, as well as relevant Policies and Procedures, or which may be assigned to them from time to time by the Council. The Secretary shall be responsible for Council meeting agendas and notes, for the archiving of these and other legal documents, and for managing the seal of the organization.

3) **Treasurer**

The Treasurer shall perform all duties as may be required by law, by the Articles of Incorporation, or by these Bylaws, or which may be prescribed from time to time by the Council in accordance with the Policies and Procedures. The Treasurer shall be the bookkeeper for the corporation in the event that professional staff are not hired for that role.

4) **Past Chair**

Past Chairs shall serve as ex officio, non-voting members of the Council for one year following the conclusion of their most recent term as Chair, provided they are not continuing as a voting Council member. If a Chair is re-elected to the Council, this *ex officio* status shall not take effect until they no longer serve as a voting Council member.

7. ## Vacancies {#vacancies}

In the event of a vacancy on the Council, remaining Councilors may appoint interim Councilors to serve for the remainder of the full term of the Councilor for which the vacancy was created or occurred and until such Councilor’s successor shall have been elected and qualified; *provided, however*, that in the event of a vacancy resulting in fewer Councilors than the minimum of three (3) required by CNCL, the following procedures should be followed:

1) **Temporary Suspension of Certain Decisions**: No major decisions (such as bylaw amendments or financial commitments) may be made until the minimum number of Councilors is restored.

   2) **Emergency Appointment Process**: In the event of a vacancy resulting in fewer than the minimum number of Councilors, remaining Council members may appoint interim Councilors until an election by Fellows can be conducted.

      3) **Election by Fellows**: The Secretary must receive nominations for new officers. These nominations shall be voted upon at the next Annual Meeting if it occurs in less than four months, or at a Special Meeting to be called for the election within four months, whichever is earlier.

If there is a vacancy for an Officer position, the Council shall appoint a qualified Councilor to fill the Officer position as soon as feasible.

8. ## Resignation & Removal {#resignation-&-removal}

Resignation from the Council must be in writing and received by the Secretary. 

**Removal by Fellows**

	Fellows entitled to vote may remove any Councilor, with or without cause, by approval of a majority of all Fellows entitled to vote, subject to any cumulative voting protections provided under California Corporations Code §5222(b).

**Removal by the Council for Cause**

The Council may remove any Councilor for cause by approval of a majority of the Councilors then in office, provided that a quorum is present, for any of the following reasons as defined in California Corporations Code §5222(a):

a. The Councilor has been declared of unsound mind by a final order of the court.

b. The Councilor has been convicted of a felony.

c. The Councilor has been found by a final order or judgment to have breached fiduciary duties to the Institute under California Corporations Code §5231.

d. The Councilor has failed to meet attendance requirements, as defined in these Bylaws or Policies, after written notice and opportunity to respond.

**Removal of Appointed Councilors**

A Councilor appointed by the Council to fill a vacancy may be removed at any time by approval of a majority of the Councilors then in office, unless the appointment was made by the Fellows or another body with the exclusive right of appointment.

**Procedure for Removal**

**1\.** Notice of the proposed removal must be given at least ten (10) days in advance of the meeting at which the removal will be considered, and must state the grounds for removal.

2\. The Councilor subject to removal shall have the opportunity to be heard before a final vote is taken.

3\. Removal becomes effective immediately upon approval unless otherwise stated in the motion for removal.

**Effect of Removal and Vacancies**

A removed Councilor’s position shall be deemed vacant as of the effective date of removal. The vacancy shall be filled in accordance with the vacancy-filling procedures in these Bylaws.

Removal shall not invalidate any prior action taken by the Council prior to the effective date of removal.

9. ## Compensation {#compensation}

Councilors are volunteers and may not be compensated for their services as Councilors, but may be reimbursed for their reasonable out-of-pocket expenses incurred in connection with the performance of their duties as Councilors. Councilors may be compensated for their personal and professional services rendered to or on behalf of the RIIS 2.0 if approved in advance by the Council and subject to compliance with RIIS 2.0’s Policies and Procedures.

10. ## Indemnification by the corporation of Councilors {#indemnification-by-the-corporation-of-councilors}

The Councilors and officers of RIIS 2.0 shall be indemnified by RIIS 2.0 to the fullest extent permissible under the laws of California.

11. ## Insurance for corporate agents {#insurance-for-corporate-agents}

Except as may be otherwise provided under provisions of law, the Council may adopt a resolution authorizing the purchase and maintenance of insurance on behalf of any agent of the corporation (including a director, officer, employee, or other agent of the corporation) against liabilities asserted against or incurred by the agent in such capacity or arising out of the agent’s status as such, whether or not the corporation would have the power to indemnify the agent against such liability under the Articles of Incorporation, these Bylaws, or provisions of law.

8. # **MEETINGS OF THE COUNCIL** {#meetings-of-the-council}

   1. ## Regular Meetings {#regular-meetings}

The Council shall meet at least quarterly.

2. ## Special Meetings {#special-meetings-1}

Special meetings may be called by the Chair or a simple majority of Councilors.

3. ## Quorum {#quorum}

A quorum of Council shall consist of at least two-thirds of the number of Councilors currently authorized by the Council pursuant to these Bylaws. Except as otherwise provided under the Articles of Incorporation, these Bylaws, or provisions of law, no business shall be considered by the Council at any meeting at which the required quorum is not present, and the only motion which the Chair shall entertain at such meeting is a motion to adjourn; provided, however, that a meeting at which a quorum is initially present may continue to transact business, notwithstanding the withdrawal of councilors from the meeting, if any action taken is approved by at least a majority of the required quorum for that meeting pursuant to the provisions of Section 4 below.

4. ## Consensus Decision Making  {#consensus-decision-making}

Unless otherwise required by California Nonprofit Corporation Law, the Articles of Incorporation, Article VIII, Section 5, or other provisions of these Bylaws, all decisions made by the Councilors shall be made through the consent of every Councilor present at a meeting duly held at which a quorum is present. Councilors present at a meeting at which a matter is being decided shall be given the opportunity to ask questions and voice opinions. After all questions have been answered, the individual facilitating the meeting will ask if there are objections. A Councilor may object to a decision on the grounds that it violates or may violate a fiduciary duty, will result in irreparable harm to the Corporation, or is inconsistent with the mission of the Corporation. A decision will be adopted if no Councilors object to it. If a consensus is not achieved or the Council decides via the Consensus Decision Making procedure to vote, then that act or decision should be done via a vote as per Section 5\.

5. ## Voting

Any act taken or decision made by a vote of the majority of the Councilors present at a meeting duly held at which a quorum is present is the act of the Council, unless a greater number is expressly required by the CNCL, the Articles of Incorporation, or these Bylaws. For avoidance of doubt, the following actions shall require a vote by a majority of all Councilors then in office, rather than simply a majority of the quorum, in order to be effective: 

1) Approval of contracts or transactions in which a Councilor has a direct or indirect material financial interest (provided that the vote of any interested Councilor(s) is not counted); 

   2) Creation of, and appointment to, committees exercising the authority of the Council; and 

      3) Removal of a Councilor for noncompliance with the qualifications set forth in these Bylaws as described in Article VII, Section 8 (provided that only the vote of qualified councilors is counted).

   6. ## Action without Meeting {#action-without-meeting}

Any action permitted to be taken at a meeting of the Council may be taken without a meeting if all members of the Council entitled to vote thereon consent in writing to the action and the number of Councilors then in office constitutes a quorum. 

7. ## Conduct of Meetings {#conduct-of-meetings}

Meetings of the Council shall be presided over by the Chair of the Council, or, in their absence, by an Officer designated by a majority of the Councilors present at the meeting. The Secretary shall act as secretary of all meetings of the Council, *provided* that, in their absence, the presiding officer shall appoint another person to act as the secretary of the meeting. Meetings shall follow the Policies and Procedures of RIIS 2.0 

9. #      **WORKING GROUPS** {#working-groups}

   1. ## Types of Working Groups {#types-of-working-groups}

There will be several standing Working Groups. Initially, these will include Finance, Infrastructure, Affiliation, Volunteer Coordination, and Communications. Standing Working Groups shall include at least one Councilor; however, the Council will have discretion to relax this requirement for specific Working Groups if needed. 

The Council may also create ad-hoc Working Groups and appoint their chairpersons and Council liaisons as needed. Ad-hoc Working Groups must be chaired by a Fellow. For avoidance of doubt, Working Groups are not “committees exercising the authority of the board” for purposes of CNCL Section 5212\. 

2. ## Meetings and Action of Working Groups {#meetings-and-action-of-working-groups}

Each Working Group’s role is to support the Council by providing expertise, ensuring efficient management of specific functions (outlined by each Working Group’s remit), and providing recommendations to the Council on major decisions needed, while the Council makes the final decisions and maintains overall oversight of the organization. See RIIS 2.0 Policies and Procedures. Each Working Group will have a set of responsibilities outlined by the Council in its Policies and Procedures, and may perform these responsibilities without Council approval, but must keep the Council apprised of activities via meeting notes or via the Councilor serving on the Working Group.

3. ## Limitations on Working Groups’ Authority {#limitations-on-working-groups’-authority}

Notwithstanding any provision to the contrary in these Bylaws, no Working Group shall have the authority to:

1) Approve any action for which the CNCL, the Articles of Incorporation, or these Bylaws require approval by the governing body of the corporation.

   2) Fill vacancies on the Council.

      3) Amend or repeal the Bylaws or adopt new Bylaws.

      4) Amend or repeal any resolution of the Council which by its express terms is not so amendable or able to be repealed.

      5) Appoint additional Working Groups or other committees of the Council or the members thereof.

      6) Expend corporate funds to support a nominee for Council after there are more people nominated for a Council seat than can be elected.

      7) Approve any self-dealing transaction, except as provided in Section 5233(d)(3) of the CNCL.

10. # **COMPENSATION APPROVAL POLICIES** {#compensation-approval-policies}

    1. ## Voting on Matters Pertaining to Compensation of Fellows: {#voting-on-matters-pertaining-to-compensation-of-fellows:}

A Councilor who is also a contractor of RIIS 2.0 is precluded from the decision-making process on matters pertaining to that Fellow’s consultant fees.

The outcomes of such decisions will be made available to Fellows within a reasonable time.

2. ## Approving Compensation {#approving-compensation}

When approving compensation for employees, contractors, and any other compensation contract or arrangement, in addition to complying with the RIIS 2.0 Policies and Procedures, the Council or its designee shall also comply with the following additional requirements:

1) **Terms of Compensation.**

The terms of compensation shall be approved by the Council prior to the first payment of compensation.

2) **Foundation of Compensation.**

The Council shall obtain and rely upon appropriate data as defined by current US Tax Code regulations for compatibility prior to approving the terms of compensation.

3) **Recording of Compensation Terms.**

The terms of compensation and the basis for approving them shall be recorded in written minutes of the meeting of the Council that approved the compensation according to the RIIS 2.0 Policies and Procedures.

11. #   **TRANSACTIONS BETWEEN RIIS 2.0 AND COUNCILORS OR OFFICERS; DISCLOSURE OF CONFLICTS OF INTEREST** {#transactions-between-riis-2.0-and-councilors-or-officers;-disclosure-of-conflicts-of-interest}

    1. ## Transactions with Councilors and Officers {#transactions-with-councilors-and-officers}

       1) **Interested Party Transactions.**

Except as described in Section 2 of this Article, the Corporation shall not be a party to any transaction:

1) in which one or more of its Councilors or Officers has a material financial interest, or 

   2) with any corporation, firm, association, or other entity in which one or more Councilors or Officers have a material financial interest.

      2) **Requirements to Authorize Interested Party Transactions.** 

RIIS 2.0 shall not be a party to any transaction described in Section 1 of this Article unless:

1) RIIS 2.0 enters into the transaction for its own benefit; 

   2) The transaction is fair and reasonable to RIIS 2.0 at the time the transaction is entered into; 

      3) prior to consummating the transaction or any part thereof, the Council authorizes or approves the transaction in good faith, by a vote of a majority of Councilors then in office (without counting the vote of the interested Councilors), and with knowledge of the material facts concerning the transaction and the interested Councilor’s or Officer’s financial interest in the transaction; 

      3) Prior to authorizing or approving the transaction, the Council considers and, in good faith, determines after reasonable investigation that RIIS 2.0 could not obtain a more advantageous arrangement with reasonable effort under the circumstances; and 

      4) The minutes of the Council meeting at which such action was taken reflect that the Council considered and made the findings described in paragraphs (a) through (d) of this Section 2\. 

12. #     **EXECUTION OF INSTRUMENTS, DEPOSITS, AND FUNDS** {#execution-of-instruments,-deposits,-and-funds}

    1. ## Execution of Instruments {#execution-of-instruments}

The Council, except as otherwise provided in these Bylaws, may by resolution authorize any Officer or agent of RIIS 2.0 to enter into any contract or execute and deliver any instrument in the name of and on behalf of RIIS 2.0, and such authority may be general or confined to specific instances. Unless so authorized, no Officer, agent, or employee shall have any power or authority to bind RIIS 2.0 by any contract or engagement to pledge its credit or to render it liable monetarily for any purpose or in any amount. 

13. # **CORPORATE RECORDS, REPORTS, AND SEAL** {#corporate-records,-reports,-and-seal}

    1. ## Maintenance of Corporate Records {#maintenance-of-corporate-records}

RIIS 2.0 shall endeavor to keep a secure platform accessible to Fellows:

1) **Financial Records**

The Treasurer shall ensure adequate and correct books and records of account, including accounts of its properties and business transactions and accounts of its assets, liabilities, receipts, disbursements, gains, and losses, as outlined in the RIIS 2.0 Policies and Procedures.

2) **Corporate Documents**

The Secretary shall ensure that a copy of the corporation’s Articles of Incorporation and Bylaws, as amended to date, is open to inspection by the Fellows and made available on a secured section of the RIIS 2.0 website.

3) **Meeting Records and Minutes**

The Secretary shall, in accordance with the provisions of these Bylaws or as required by law, and as per RIIS 2.0 Policies and Procedures:

1) Ensure that the minutes of all meetings of Councilors, Working Groups, and of all Annual or Special Meetings of Fellows are properly maintained.

   2) Ensure that minutes of meetings, written consents approving action without a meeting, and any supporting documents are contemporaneously recorded in the corporate records.

      3) See that all notices are duly given in accordance with the provisions of these Bylaws and policies, or as required by law.

         4) Make Council meeting summaries available to Fellows after approval by the Council.

         5) Record of any votes taken in connection with the proceedings.

         6) Act as custodian of:

            1) The corporate records of this organization.

            2) An affiliate database containing the name of every Fellow and Associate, including termination dates for any memberships or affiliations that have ceased.

      4) **Conflict of Interest Documentation**

The minutes of meetings of the Council and all working groups with Council-delegated powers shall contain:

1) The names of persons who disclosed or were found to have a financial interest or other possible conflict of interest;

   2) The nature of the possible conflict of interest; and

      3) The Council’s or Working Group’s decision as to whether a conflict of interest existed.

      5) **Timeline Requirements**

         1) Council resolutions should be made available to Fellows within 30 days after approval of the minutes.

         2) Minutes must be submitted to the Council for review within 30 days of the meeting.

         3) The minutes must be reviewed and approved by the Council at the next meeting, or otherwise within 30 days of the meeting to which they pertain.

      6) **Inspection Rights**

         1) Every current Councilor shall have the absolute right at any reasonable time to inspect all books, records, and documents of every kind, and shall have such other rights to inspect the books, records, and properties of this corporation as may be required under the Articles of Incorporation, other provisions of these Bylaws, and provisions of law.

         2) Every current Fellow shall have certain inspection rights, for a purpose reasonably related to such person’s interest as a Fellow. This includes the right to inspect at any reasonable time the books, records, or minutes of proceedings of the Fellows or of the Council or Working Groups of the Council, upon written demand on the secretary of the corporation by the Fellow, for a purpose reasonably related to such person’s interests as a Fellow. Fellows shall have such other rights to inspect the books and records of this corporation as may be required under the Articles of Incorporation, other provisions of these Bylaws, and provisions of law.

14. #        **COMPLIANCE WITH IRC 501(C)(3) TAX EXEMPTION PROVISIONS** {#compliance-with-irc-501(c)(3)-tax-exemption-provisions}

    1. ## Limitations on Activities {#limitations-on-activities}

No substantial part of the activities of this corporation shall be the carrying on of propaganda, or otherwise attempting to influence legislation (except as otherwise provided by Section 501(h) of the Internal Revenue Code), and this corporation shall not participate in, or intervene in (including the publishing or distribution of statements), any political campaign on behalf of, or in opposition to, any candidate for public office.

Notwithstanding any other provisions of these Bylaws, this corporation shall not carry on any activities not permitted to be carried on (a) by a corporation exempt from federal income tax under Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code, or (b) by a corporation, contributions to which are deductible under Section 170(c)(2) of the Internal Revenue Code.

2. ## Prohibition Against Private Inurement {#prohibition-against-private-inurement}

No part of the net earnings of this corporation shall inure to the benefit of, or be distributable to, its Fellows, Councilors, or trustees, officers, or other private persons, except that the corporation shall be authorized and empowered to pay reasonable compensation for services rendered and to make payments and distributions in furtherance of the purposes of this corporation.

3. ## Distribution of Assets {#distribution-of-assets}

Upon the dissolution of this corporation, its assets remaining after payment, or provision for payment, of all debts and liabilities of this corporation, shall be distributed for one or more exempt purposes within the meaning of Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code or shall be distributed to the federal government, to a state or local government, or another nonprofit for a public purpose. Such distribution shall be made in accordance with all applicable provisions of the state of California laws.

15. # **AMENDMENTS** {#amendments}

    1. ## Proposal Submission Requirements {#proposal-submission-requirements}

Amendments to these Bylaws may be proposed by: (a) at least two-thirds majority of the Council at a meeting duly held at which a quorum is present, or (b) a petition signed by not less than fifteen percent (15%) of the Fellows.

Proposed amendments must be submitted to the Secretary to be included on the agenda for the next regular Council meeting and must be sent to all Councilors with regular Council meeting materials at least 14 days prior to the meeting.

Any proposed amendment to these Bylaws must be submitted in writing and should include:

1) The exact text of the existing bylaw section(s) to be amended

   2) The exact text of the proposed amendment

      3) A brief explanation of the purpose and effect of the proposed amendment

      4) The name(s) of the proposer(s)

   2. ## Review Process {#review-process}

Prior to voting, all proposed amendments shall be reviewed by the Council, which shall provide a written recommendation to the Fellows regarding the proposed amendment’s consistency with existing Bylaws, compliance with applicable laws, and alignment with the organization’s mission and values.

3. ## Ratification Criteria {#ratification-criteria}

To become effective, the amendment must receive affirmative votes of at least two-thirds of the votes cast by Fellows, *provided* that voting quorum of Fellows is achieved as defined in Article IV, Section 4\. 

4. ## Ratification Process {#ratification-process}

Amendments approved by the Council shall be presented to the Fellows at the next Annual Meeting or Special Meeting called for such purpose and shall require ratification as described in Section 3\. For greater certainty, amendments to these Bylaws may be ratified without Council approval, provided the Ratification Criteria are adhered to and that the amendment does not conflict with California or federal law.

5. ## Emergency Amendments {#emergency-amendments}

In case of emergency, as defined in CNCL Section 5140(n), the Council may amend these Bylaws by a three-fourths majority vote without prior notice, *provided* that such amendments are temporary and shall expire after \[90\] days unless ratified through the standard amendment process described in Sections 1-5. In addition, such emergency amendments may not modify or impact the rights of Fellows or Affiliates.

6. ## Notification and Record-Keeping {#notification-and-record-keeping}

Upon ratification of any amendment, the Secretary shall:

1) Update the official copy of the Bylaws.

   2) Publish the amended Bylaws on the secure RIIS 2.0 website within 7 days and notify all Fellows of the amendment via electronic communication.

      3) Maintain a record of all amendments, including dates of proposal, approval, and ratification.

   7. ## Periodic Review {#periodic-review}

The Bylaws shall be reviewed in their entirety by the Council at least once every 2 years, with recommendations for updates or amendments presented to the Council as appropriate.

16. # **CONSTRUCTION AND TERMS** {#construction-and-terms}

If there is any conflict between the provisions of these Bylaws and the Articles of Incorporation of this non-profit corporation, the provisions of the Articles of Incorporation shall govern.

Should any of the provisions or portions of these Bylaws be held unenforceable or invalid for any reason, the remaining provisions and portions of these Bylaws shall be unaffected by such holding.

All references in these Bylaws to the Articles of Incorporation shall be to the articles of incorporation, articles of organization, certificate of incorporation, or other founding document of RIIS 2.0, as currently in effect, filed with an office of the State of California and used to establish the legal existence of this corporation.

All references in these Bylaws to a section or sections of the Internal Revenue Code shall be to such sections of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 as amended from time to time or to corresponding provisions of any future federal tax code.

